The paper supports a native edge-matching family: tiles are the solver’s objects, rotations are allowed, border color 0 must face outward, and exact verification is just permutation/orientation plus edge comparisons. Its key triage result is sobering but usable: full MILP and max-clique methods solve through 6×6, while 7×7 and larger were not completed in the reported runs; easy subproblems include fixed-position rotation, border optimization, and non-adjacent reassignment by Hungarian matching. I’m now testing whether an inverse-generated distribution can avoid those easy signatures and withstand a CSP backtracker at a genuinely hard preset.
